# Copy to .env; both backend services read it, and so do `yarn e2e` and
# `yarn dev`, which go through them.

# Where the account API keeps its data, in Npgsql's key=value form (a
# `postgres://` URL is not one it reads). This value matches compose.yaml, so
# it is the right one after `yarn db:start`.
DATABASE_URL=Host=localhost;Port=5432;Username=postgres;Password=postgres;Database=frontend_demo

# Where the browser reports errors and traces. Unset means Sentry stays off
# (see src/sentry.ts), which is what `yarn dev` and the browser tests want.
# Project: alexisregnaud/demo-web-frontend
# VITE_SENTRY_DSN=https://aa1392465e90500f985f79b4ac815529@o4509266977554432.ingest.de.sentry.io/4512137323085904

# Datadog RUM and browser logs, beside Sentry rather than instead of it (see
# src/datadog.ts). Off unless both the application id and the client token are
# set. They come from Digital Experience > Add an application > JS in Datadog;
# the token is public by design, like a DSN. SITE is your organisation's site -
# datadoghq.eu for an EU account - and defaults to the US one.
# VITE_DATADOG_APPLICATION_ID=
# VITE_DATADOG_CLIENT_TOKEN=
# VITE_DATADOG_SITE=datadoghq.eu
#
# An API key - Organization Settings > API Keys, NOT the client token above -
# turns `yarn build` into a source-map upload to Datadog, beside Sentry's.
# Secret, unlike the token. See vite.base.ts.
# DATADOG_API_KEY=

# One Sentry project per remote, if you want one. The app is three builds
# (docs/adr/0003), and by default all three report into the browser project
# above. Set a remote's DSN here and its build uploads its maps to, and its
# errors are routed to, a project of its own - named demo-web-<remote>-frontend,
# which has to exist in Sentry first (see vite.base.ts). An error thrown in
# the account's preview on the home page is then the account team's, wherever
# it was rendered: src/sentry-owner.ts reads the owner off the stack. Only the
# builds read these; the browser learns them from the bundle.
# Project: alexisregnaud/demo-web-account-frontend
# SENTRY_DSN_ACCOUNT=
# Project: alexisregnaud/demo-web-home-frontend
# SENTRY_DSN_HOME=

# Uploads the production source maps to Sentry on `yarn build`, so a stack trace
# names your code instead of the minified bundle. Unset means no map is even
# generated (see vite.config.ts). The same variable does the .NET half on
# `yarn server:build` - the PDBs, which carry their own sources, so an API frame
# shows the line too (see server/Accounts/Accounts.csproj). Mint one at
# https://alexisregnaud.sentry.io/settings/auth-tokens/, which issues an
# ORGANIZATION token (`sntrys_...`) - no scopes to pick, and it outlives whoever
# minted it. The browser's maps would take a personal token too; the .NET debug
# files would not, so one organization token is what covers both.
#
# The notifications service needs NEITHER, and that is worth knowing before you
# go looking for its maps: it runs its TypeScript through Node's type stripping
# with no build step, so a stack frame already names the real line, and
# @sentry/node reads the file off the disk to show the code around it. It has no
# artifact to upload - only the source tree it is running, which a deployment
# has to actually ship for this to keep working.
# SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=

# Where the API reports errors and traces. A DIFFERENT Sentry project from the
# browser's, so a .NET stack and a React stack land in separate issue streams -
# the two halves of one request still read as ONE trace, because a trace is
# linked by its id and not by its project. Unset means the API stays off (see
# server/Accounts/Program.cs), which is what CI wants. Set here, it applies to
# `yarn e2e` too - Playwright starts the API with `yarn accounts:start`, and that
# reads this file.
# Project: alexisregnaud/demo-account-backend
# SENTRY_DSN=

# Where the NOTIFICATIONS service reports - the second backend process, which
# the account API calls when an account changes (docs/adr/0004) and which runs
# on Node rather than .NET (docs/adr/0005). Its own Sentry project, so its
# stacks are its own team's, the way a remote's are above; unset it falls back
# to SENTRY_DSN, so the demo is worth running with one DSN set. The project has
# to exist in Sentry first.
#
# Either way the TRACE is one. A browser pageload, the account API's PUT and
# this service's POST all carry the same trace id, because a trace is linked by
# its id and not by its project - and, since this service became Node
# (docs/adr/0005), not by its runtime either. What tells the two services apart
# when they share a DSN is the `service` tag, set in
# server/Accounts/SentrySetup.cs on one side and
# server/Notifications/instrument.ts on the other.
# Project: alexisregnaud/demo-notifications-backend
# SENTRY_DSN_NOTIFICATIONS=

# Where the two backend services are. Defaults - port 3002 for the notifications
# service, and the URL the account API calls it at - match what `yarn start` and
# Playwright run, so neither needs setting locally. A real deployment sets
# NOTIFICATIONS_URL to wherever that service is, which is the backend's version
# of `remoteEntryUrl` in federation.config.ts: the address of a deployment you
# depend on, written down where you can see it.
# NOTIFICATIONS_PORT=3002
# NOTIFICATIONS_URL=http://localhost:3002

# WHICH BUILD every part reports as. The browser's three builds are set for
# you - the Vite plugin names them after the git commit and stamps the name
# into each bundle, and .github/workflows/ci.yml passes the same sha in
# explicitly. The two backend services read it HERE instead, at startup,
# because nothing bakes it into them.
#
# They do not degrade the same way when it is unset. The account API falls back
# to the name .NET builds out of its assembly version - `Accounts@1.0.0+<sha>`,
# which is right but spelled differently from the browser's bare sha, so a
# search for one release finds one part of the request and not the others. The
# notifications service reports NO release at all: Node has neither an assembly
# attribute nor a bundler to stamp one in (see server/Notifications/instrument.ts).
#
# Locally, unset is fine; the parts just stop lining up in a release comparison.
# A real deployment should carry the sha INTO each backend's artifact - a
# Dockerfile `ARG` turned `ENV`, say - rather than set it from the platform's
# own config, so that a rollback cannot leave a process reporting the release of
# code it is no longer running.
# SENTRY_RELEASE=

# The Lighthouse CI project `yarn lighthouse` uploads to, on the server
# `yarn lhci:start` runs. Its BUILD token, which only lets a run add results:
# `yarn lhci wizard` creates the project against http://localhost:9001 and
# prints it once, next to an admin token that belongs in a password manager,
# not here. `yarn lighthouse:pull` uses the same one to bring CI's runs in.
# LHCI_TOKEN=
